# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL: rendered-site testing stack (visual and accessibility), for
# rendered testing in the sidecar.
## Prompt: Browser checks: lets the agent open pages in a browser inside the sidecar, take screenshots and run axe accessibility scans, with Playwright. Pages that load web fonts also need each font host added to .ddev/claude/egress-domains.list.
## Question: Should the agent be able to check pages in a browser?
#
## Needs: Node 20 or newer from the baseline above; Playwright does not load on
## an older one, and the check below stops the build on it.
##
## Egress: rendered pages match production only if the site's web fonts load,
## so this block needs one line per web-font host the site's pages request,
## and no other host, in .ddev/claude/egress-domains.list, the firewall's only
## domain list. The site's analytics and help widgets stay blocked, and every
## line added is egress surface.
##
## Lets the sidecar serve the site to itself (`drush runserver
## 127.0.0.1:PORT`) and drive it with headless Chromium for screenshots and
## axe-core scans, with no route to the web container. Installed at build
## because the runtime firewall and the npm-registry deny block runtime
## installs; the build runs on the Docker builder with open network. Chromium
## must be launched with --no-sandbox --disable-dev-shm-usage in this
## container.
##
## The browser is Debian's chromium at /usr/bin/chromium, verified by apt's
## signatures. Playwright fetches no browser of its own, so scripts launch with
## executablePath: '/usr/bin/chromium'. Playwright only guarantees the Chromium
## build it bundles, so a Debian chromium far from that build can break
## individual APIs.
##
## The npm packages are verified by HTTPS and npm's integrity check against the
## registry, not by a pinned checksum. One version pins both playwright and
## @playwright/test, because the runner and the library share playwright-core
## and a mismatch fails confusingly rather than loudly.
# ARG PLAYWRIGHT_VERSION=1.64.0
# ARG AXE_PLAYWRIGHT_VERSION=4.13.0
# ENV NODE_PATH=${NODE_PATH:+${NODE_PATH}:}/opt/render-tools/node_modules
# RUN major="$(node -p 'process.versions.node.split(".")[0]')" \
#  && if [ "${major}" -lt 20 ]; then /usr/local/lib/agent-container-kit/fix-it.sh build-fragment-node-too-old block="rendered-site testing" minimum="20" found="${major}" >&2; exit 1; fi \
#  && apt-get update \
#  && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends chromium \
#  && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* \
#  && mkdir -p /opt/render-tools \
#  && cd /opt/render-tools \
#  && npm init -y >/dev/null 2>&1 \
#  && npm install --no-fund --no-audit --save-exact \
#       "playwright@${PLAYWRIGHT_VERSION}" \
#       "@playwright/test@${PLAYWRIGHT_VERSION}" \
#       "@axe-core/playwright@${AXE_PLAYWRIGHT_VERSION}" \
#  && chmod -R a+rX /opt/render-tools

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL: further accessibility checks, beyond axe-core from the render
# block.
## Prompt: More accessibility checkers: Lighthouse CI, pa11y-ci, a virtual screen reader and stylelint-a11y, on top of the browser checks.
## Question: Should the agent have these extra accessibility checkers?
#
## Needs: the render block above, for its chromium and /opt/render-tools.
##
## @guidepup/virtual-screen-reader reports what a screen reader would
## announce, not only whether the ARIA is present. It runs over the DOM, a
## Playwright page included, with no real assistive technology.
##
## Lighthouse CI and pa11y-ci largely overlap axe in rule coverage; they add a
## second opinion and a score gate. They use Debian's chromium through
## CHROME_PATH (Lighthouse CI) and PUPPETEER_EXECUTABLE_PATH (pa11y's
## Puppeteer), and PUPPETEER_SKIP_DOWNLOAD stops Puppeteer fetching a browser
## at install.
##
## stylelint-a11y is not part of core's stylelint ruleset, so it runs as its
## own pass with its own config and installs apart from core's lint set;
## folding it into core's ruleset would make local results stop matching
## drupal.org CI.
##
## These packages are verified by HTTPS and npm's integrity check against the
## registry, not by a pinned checksum. They install the release current when this layer is
## built, and Docker's build cache keeps that release until the cache is
## skipped (`ddev restart --no-cache`).
# ENV CHROME_PATH=/usr/bin/chromium
# ENV PUPPETEER_EXECUTABLE_PATH=/usr/bin/chromium
# RUN if [ ! -d /opt/render-tools ]; then /usr/local/lib/agent-container-kit/fix-it.sh build-fragment-block-needs block="further accessibility checks" needs="rendered-site testing" >&2; exit 1; fi \
#  && cd /opt/render-tools \
#  && npm install --no-fund --no-audit @guidepup/virtual-screen-reader \
#  && chmod -R a+rX /opt/render-tools \
#  && mkdir -p /opt/a11y-tools \
#  && cd /opt/a11y-tools \
#  && npm init -y >/dev/null 2>&1 \
#  && PUPPETEER_SKIP_DOWNLOAD=true npm install --no-fund --no-audit @lhci/cli pa11y-ci stylelint-a11y \
#  && ln -sf /opt/a11y-tools/node_modules/.bin/lhci /usr/local/bin/lhci \
#  && ln -sf /opt/a11y-tools/node_modules/.bin/pa11y-ci /usr/local/bin/pa11y-ci \
#  && chmod -R a+rX /opt/a11y-tools

